module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Algebraic.Basic
public import Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination

/-!
# The automorphism form of Tag 04LP is false

Tag 04LP asserts, for a groupoid scheme `(U, R, s, t, c)` over `S` with `U = Spec k` and `k` a
field, that any two points `r, r'` of `R` are related as follows: there are a field extension
`k'/k`, points `r₁, r₂` of `R ×_{s, Spec k} Spec k'` and an isomorphism `φ` of
`R ×_{s, Spec k} Spec k'` *with itself* over `Spec k'` such that `φ(r₁) = r₂`,
`pr₀(r₁) = r` and `pr₀(r₂) = r'`.  Because the same base change appears on both sides of `φ`
with a single field extension, `φ` is an automorphism.

That statement is **false**.  Its proof in the source is the single line "this is a special case
of Lemma 02YF parts (1) and (2)", and Tag 02YF produces *two* embeddings `k → k'` — one induced
by the target and one by the source of a chosen arrow — together with an isomorphism between the
two corresponding base changes.  Writing one symbol for both base changes conflates the two
embeddings, and they are not conjugate in general.  The weaker two-embedding statement that the
cited proof does establish is
`Stacks.Groupoids.GroupoidScheme.exists_fieldExtension_sourceFiber_iso_mapping_points`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Groupoids.linear_ne_zero_of_transcendental`: a nonzero `L`-linear form in a
  transcendental element is nonzero.
* `Stacks.Groupoids.sq_linear_ne_mul_sq_linear_of_transcendental`: if `c` is transcendental over
  `L` and `a f - b e ≠ 0` in `L`, then `(a c + b) ^ 2 ≠ c (e c + f) ^ 2`.
* `Stacks.Groupoids.linearFractional_sq_ne_of_transcendental`: equivalently, no fractional linear
  transformation with coefficients in `L` carries a transcendental `c` to a square root of `c`.

The last two are the arithmetic heart of the counterexample described below: there the group of
automorphisms available to `φ` is the group of fractional linear transformations with
coefficients algebraic over `ℚ`, `r₁` is forced to be the point with coordinate `c`, and every
candidate `r₂` has coordinate a square root of `c`.

## Implementation notes

### The counterexample

Take `S = Spec ℚ`, `k = ℚ(T)`, `U = Spec k`, and for `R` the *coarse* groupoid
`R = U ×_S U = Spec (k ⊗_ℚ k)`, with `t = pr₀`, `s = pr₁` (the conventions of Tag 022P for
`j = id`), composition `(x₀, x₁) ∘ (x₁, x₂) = (x₀, x₂)`, identity the diagonal and inverse the
swap.  The identity `j = id : U ×_S U → U ×_S U` is an equivalence relation on `U` over `S`,
because on `T`-valued points it is the full relation on `U(T)`; so by Tag 0233 it extends,
uniquely, to a groupoid scheme over `S`, and that groupoid is the one just described.  Its object
scheme is the spectrum of a field, so Tag 04LP applies to it verbatim.

Write `B = k ⊗_ℚ k`, `u = T ⊗ 1` and `v = 1 ⊗ T`, so that `t` is dual to `T ↦ u` and `s` to
`T ↦ v`, and `B` is `ℚ(u)[v]` with every nonzero element of `ℚ[v]` inverted.  The two points of
`R` we use are the maximal ideals
`r = (v - u) B` (the image of the identity section) and `r' = (v - u²) B`;
both have residue field `ℚ(u)`, since `B / (v - u) B ≅ ℚ(u)` by `v ↦ u` and
`B / (v - u²) B ≅ ℚ(u)` by `v ↦ u²`, the inverted elements of `ℚ[v]` having nonzero image in
both cases because `u` is transcendental over `ℚ`.

Now let `k'/k` be *any* field extension, with structure map `γ : k → k'`, and set
`c = γ(T) ∈ k'`; as `γ` is injective on `ℚ(T)`, the element `c` is transcendental over `ℚ`.
Since `B ⊗_{s, k, γ} k' = (k ⊗_ℚ k) ⊗_k k' ≅ k ⊗_ℚ k'`, the base change is
`X = R ×_{s, Spec k} Spec k' = Spec A` with
`A = k ⊗_ℚ k' = k'[u]` localized at `ℚ[u] ∖ {0}`,
a domain with fraction field `k'(u)`, and `pr₀ : X → R` is dual to `u ↦ u`, `v ↦ c`.

*The point `r₁` is forced.*  A prime `P` of `A` lying over `r` contains the image `c - u` of
`v - u`, hence contains `(u - c) A`, which is maximal because `A / (u - c) A ≅ k'` (again `c` is
transcendental over `ℚ`, so no inverted element of `ℚ[u]` dies).  Conversely `(u - c) A` does lie
over `r`, because `B → A → k'` sends `u, v ↦ c` and so factors as `B → B / (v - u) ≅ ℚ(u) → k'`,
`u ↦ c`, which is injective.  Thus `pr₀⁻¹(r) = {(u - c) A}`: the unique `k'`-rational point with
coordinate `u = c`.

*Every candidate `r₂` has coordinate a square root of `c`.*  A prime of `A` lying over `r'`
contains the image `c - u²` of `v - u²`, hence contains `(u² - c) A`; so it is generated by an
irreducible factor of `u² - c` in `k'[u]`, and its points over an algebraic closure `Ω` of `k'`
are roots of `u² = c`.

*The automorphism group of `X` over `Spec k'` is small.*  A `k'`-algebra automorphism of `A`
extends uniquely to a `k'`-automorphism of `Frac A = k'(u)`, and those form `PGL₂(k')` acting by
fractional linear substitutions in `u`.  As `A` is a localization of the principal ideal domain
`k'[u]`, we have `A = ⋂ 𝒪_x` over the closed points `x` of `ℙ¹_{k'}` with `A ⊆ 𝒪_x`, and that
set of points is `ℙ¹_{k'} ∖ Z`, where `Z` consists of the point at infinity (excluded because
`u ∈ A`) together with the primes dividing some nonzero element of `ℚ[u]` (excluded because the
reciprocal of that element lies in `A`).  Since `{x : A ⊆ 𝒪_x}` is intrinsic to the subring
`A ⊆ k'(u)`, any such automorphism permutes it and hence preserves `Z`.  On `Ω`-points,
`Z` is exactly `ℙ¹(ℚ̄)`, where `ℚ̄` is the algebraic closure of `ℚ` inside `Ω`: the points of `Z`
are the point at infinity and the roots of the nonzero elements of `ℚ[u]`.  A fractional linear
transformation preserving `ℙ¹(ℚ̄)` sends the three points `0, 1, ∞` of `ℙ¹(ℚ̄)` to three distinct
points of `ℙ¹(ℚ̄)` and is therefore itself defined over `ℚ̄`; being also defined over `k'`, after
normalizing a representative matrix by one of its nonzero entries it is defined over
`L = ℚ̄ ∩ k'`, the subfield of elements of `k'` algebraic over `ℚ`.  So every automorphism of `X`
over `Spec k'` acts on coordinates as `u ↦ (a u + b) / (e u + f)` with `a, b, e, f ∈ L` and
`a f - b e ≠ 0`.

*Conclusion.*  Such an automorphism sends `r₁`, the point with coordinate `c`, to the point with
coordinate `(a c + b) / (e c + f)`, and `φ(r₁) = r₂` would force
`((a c + b) / (e c + f)) ^ 2 = c`.  Since `L` is algebraic over `ℚ` and `c` is transcendental
over `ℚ`, the element `c` is transcendental over `L`, and
`linearFractional_sq_ne_of_transcendental` says exactly that this is impossible.  Hence no
field extension `k'/k`, no points `r₁, r₂` and no automorphism `φ` as in Tag 04LP exist for this
groupoid scheme and this pair of points.

Note that the counterexample requires `k` to be transcendental over the subfield of `S` it comes
from.  For `k/k₀` finite separable the coarse groupoid satisfies the automorphism form, because
after extending to a splitting field the base change becomes a finite disjoint union of copies of
`Spec k'` and every permutation of the pieces is an automorphism over `Spec k'`.

### What is and is not formalized here

Only the arithmetic obstruction is formalized.  The surrounding steps — that the coarse groupoid
is a groupoid scheme, the identification of the base change with `Spec (k ⊗_ℚ k')`, the two prime
computations, and the determination of the automorphism group — are standard commutative algebra
whose formalization would require a large amount of infrastructure that the conclusion does not
warrant; they are recorded above as a mathematical argument and are not checked by the kernel.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 022P](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/022P)
